      {
        "id": 91342,
        "name": "Arcanist Lupus",
        "username": "Arcanist_Lupus",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arcanist_lupus/{size}/2169_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-02-15T06:34:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Spiff\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/spiff/48/1865_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Spiff:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Maybe the flip mechanic could be that once you flip an individual V5's character card, they become another member of the V5, starting at full health.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>You'd probably need to give them some sort of advantage too. &nbsp;If the heros are all set up, while the villian has completely reset, then they'll be at a severe disadvantage, especially if the heroes' setup includes DR or deck control. &nbsp;Yes, they'll be at a bit lower life, but I don't think that'd matter much. &nbsp;I'd say give each of them a unique \"explode\" effect, e.g. \"When you kill Baron Blade, he destroys all ongoings and turns into Fright Train\", \"When you kill Fright Train, he deals each hero 10 damage and turns into Friction\".</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>This doesn't feel quite the same as fighting an individual character to me, though. &nbsp;Interesting, but not quite what we're after.</p>",
        "post_number": 27,
